 |---|---|---|
-| approved-only-if-checked | params.approved != true or target.checks_passed == true or target.wording_only == true | A commit is recorded as approved only if its change check passed, or it only changes wording. |
+| approved-only-if-checked | params.approved != true or (target.checks_passed == true and now - target.checked_at < 1 day) or target.wording_only == true | A commit is recorded as approved only if its change check passed less than a day ago, or it only changes wording. |

 **deliver** — owner. Target: Commit. Param: environment (enum: local, staging). Ask Delivery to deliver an approved commit to an environment. It triggers the handoff described below.
 Preconditions:
@@ -325,7 +326,7 @@
 - **element**: One part of a declaration (an object, a link, an action, a cell, an invariant), drafted by an agent and confirmed by a person; an agent may confirm only an element that only fixes wording (wording_only). An element about who may approve or deploy (permission) also needs a second, different person to confirm it.
 - **withdrawn**: A proposed element the owner took back because it was wrong; it leaves the draft rather than staying as unknown.
 - **draft**: The working copy of a domain's declaration on a branch: what the next commit will snapshot.
-- **change check**: The checks a commit runs before approval: structure, semantics, scenarios, harness, past actions, stranded objects. A commit marked wording_only (it only changes wording) may be approved by a person without it.
+- **change check**: (A passing check counts only while it is less than one day old.) The checks a commit runs before approval: structure, semantics, scenarios, harness, past actions, stranded objects. A commit marked wording_only (it only changes wording) may be approved by a person without it.
 - **unknown**: An element marked as not known yet, instead of guessed. It stays visible until someone answers it.

 ## Delivery

A.1.0. {"problem": "The invariant 'approved-commits-passed-their-checks' still only requires checks_passed == true (or wording_only). The change makes approval depend on the check being under a day old, but this invariant was not updated. It is now inconsistent with the approve_commit and record_approval rules. It is also unclear how it would behave as the check ages: an already-approved commit stays valid in the invariant, but a strict freshness check would flip it to failing a day after approval.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring invariants: approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.1.1. {"problem": "Examples that rely on checks_passed true without any checked_at now conflict with the new rule. 'an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name' and 'a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away' start with C2 having checks_passed true but no checked_at, so the freshness test cannot be satisfied and step 2 of the deputy example (approve allowed) would be denied. Also, the first example approves right after record_check with no mention of the time; it works only if the check is fresh, and no example covers a stale check being refused.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md: a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away, an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name, approve-only-after-a-passing-check", "kind": "reference"}
A.1.2. {"problem": "When checked_at is unset (e.g. commits whose check was recorded before this change, or fixtures), 'now - target.checked_at < 1 day' is undefined. Existing commits with checks_passed true now cannot be approved until re-checked, and the behaviour is not specified. The check also sits in the first half of the checks-pass expression, so checks_passed true with unset checked_at is ambiguous.", "where": "SKILL.md: approve_commit precondition checks-pass; record_approval precondition approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.1.3. {"problem": "The freshness rule is applied twice, at approve_commit and again at record_approval. The kernel's record_approval reply comes after the approve_commit call, so a check that ages past one day between the two could cause the approval to be refused after the kernel has already merged and tagged the commit, leaving main updated but the commit not recorded as approved. The merge is irreversible.", "where": "SKILL.md: record_approval precondition approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.1.4. {"problem": "The parenthetical was inserted at the front of the change check glossary entry in a way that reads as a stray note rather than a definition, and the glossary says a 'passing check counts' without saying it applies to approval or to wording_only commits. Separately, the 'checked_at' type is a string while the rule does arithmetic on it (now - checked_at < 1 day), with no stated time format.", "where": "SKILL.md: Authoring glossary 'change check'; Commit.checked_at property", "kind": "other"}
A.1.5. {"problem": "check_commit and record_check are not allowed on an approved commit, and approve_commit refuses an approved commit, so there is no problem there. However, a stale check requires re-running check_commit, and the check_commit precondition text and the checks-pass reason are the only places that explain this. Nothing says that a re-run overwrites checked_at, and a late record_check result on an unapproved commit can reset the time, so a failing re-check clears the earlier pass, which is not stated.", "where": "SKILL.md: check_commit / record_check", "kind": "other"}
A.2.0. {"problem": "The invariant still says an approved commit needs checks_passed == true (or wording_only) with no freshness condition. The change makes approval and record_approval require a check under one day old, but this invariant was not updated, so the rule is now inconsistent. Also, once a check ages past a day, an already approved commit still satisfies the invariant, so the invariant no longer matches the approval rule.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring invariants: approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.2.1. {"problem": "The example 'approve-only-after-a-passing-check' never exercises the new age rule. It records the check and approves, but shows no stale-check denial or re-run. Example fixtures (C2 in 'an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name' and 'a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away') have checks_passed true but no checked_at. Under the new precondition, now - checked_at cannot be evaluated or is false, so the deputy's approve_commit on C2 would be denied, though the example expects it to be allowed. Setting checks_passed by a fixture also bypasses record_check, so checked_at is never set.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md: a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away, approve-only-after-a-passing-check, an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name", "kind": "reference"}
A.2.2. {"problem": "The checked_at field is typed as a string, but the preconditions compute now - target.checked_at < 1 day, which needs a timestamp or duration type. The type and format of the time are unspecified, so the comparison is ill-defined. The behaviour when checked_at is unset is also unspecified (a commit with checks_passed true and no checked_at, e.g. any commit checked before this change). Existing checked commits would silently become unapprovable.", "where": "SKILL.md, Commit.checked_at property and checks-pass / approved-only-if-checked preconditions", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.2.3. {"problem": "The glossary edit puts the rule in a parenthetical in front of the definition, reading awkwardly. The checks-pass reason tells the user to re-run the check, but check_commit has a not-approved precondition only, so re-checking works. However record_check can overwrite checks_passed and checked_at on an unapproved commit, so a failing re-run clears an earlier pass. This is not stated anywhere. The 'checks-pass' wording also now embeds the rule in prose ('the check is less than one day old') mixed with an expression, so the check is not a cleanly evaluable expression.", "where": "SKILL.md, glossary 'change check' and approve_commit checks-pass", "kind": "other"}
A.2.4. {"problem": "The two preconditions re-evaluate time at different moments. approve_commit checks freshness when the owner acts, and record_approval checks again when the kernel replies. A check that is 23h59m old at approval can be 24h old by the time record_approval runs, so the kernel's recording is refused after approval was allowed, leaving the commit merged to main by the kernel but not recorded as approved.", "where": "SKILL.md, approve_commit checks-pass and record_approval approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.3.0. {"problem": "The invariant 'approved-commits-passed-their-checks' still only requires checks_passed == true (or wording_only). It does not include the one-day freshness rule that approve_commit and record_approval now enforce. The invariant and the preconditions now disagree. Also, a commit that was approved while its check was fresh stays approved, but the check ages out, so the stored state no longer matches what the rule says counts as checked.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring invariants: approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.3.1. {"problem": "The example 'approve-only-after-a-passing-check' no longer matches the rules. record_check now sets checked_at, but the example never mentions it. Its steps (record_check, approve, record_approval) are not checked against freshness, and no example covers a stale check (a day or older) being denied by 'checks-pass'. The start state of 'a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away' has checks_passed true with no checked_at. Under the new precondition, (checks_passed and now - checked_at < 1 day) cannot be evaluated or is false when checked_at is unset, so approval is denied and the example's expected 'allowed' is wrong. The same applies to 'an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name', whose C2 has checks_passed true and no checked_at.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md: a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away, approve-only-after-a-passing-check, an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name", "kind": "reference"}
A.3.2. {"problem": "The freshness check is re-evaluated in record_approval, where the kernel records the result of an approval that has already happened. Approval runs through the kernel and the reply arrives later. If the check turns 24 hours old between approve_commit and record_approval, the reply is refused and the kernel's approval (merge to main and tag) cannot be recorded. This leaves the kernel and the domain out of step. record_check's own 'settled' precondition and the 'not-approved' guard show that late replies are meant to be tolerated.", "where": "SKILL.md, record_approval: approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.3.3. {"problem": "The time comparison is not defined. checked_at is a string typed property, but the rules subtract it from now and compare the result to '1 day'. No format or time zone is given, and behaviour is undefined when checked_at is unset (for example commits whose check was recorded before this change). Existing commits with checks_passed true and no checked_at become unapprovable until they are re-checked.", "where": "SKILL.md, Commit.checked_at and the checks-pass / approved-only-if-checked checks", "kind": "other"}
A.3.4. {"problem": "The glossary entry for 'change check' puts the freshness rule in a parenthetical placed before the definition. It also says a wording_only commit may be approved 'without it', which is fine. But the check_commit 'not-approved' reason and the 'wording_only' text do not mention that a stale check must be re-run. Also, check_commit is blocked once the commit is approved, so the 'run it again' advice does not apply to approved commits.", "where": "SKILL.md, glossary: change check", "kind": "other"}
A.4.0. {"problem": "The invariant 'approved-commits-passed-their-checks' still only requires checks_passed == true (or wording_only). It does not include the new freshness condition. The action preconditions (checks-pass, approved-only-if-checked) now disagree with it, so the invariant no longer states the rule.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring invariants: approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.4.1. {"problem": "A commit approved while its check was fresh stays approved after the check turns a day old. Once approved, the freshness condition is no longer true of it. If the invariant were tightened to match, it would fail for every previously approved commit. The change does not say whether freshness applies only at approval time or continuously.", "where": "SKILL.md, record_approval approved-only-if-checked and the invariants", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.4.2. {"problem": "The examples now fail or are ambiguous. In 'a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away' and 'an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name', commits are set up with checks_passed true but no checked_at. The new precondition compares now - checked_at, which is undefined when checked_at is unset, so approval would be denied. The example in 'approve-only-after-a-passing-check' never shows the freshness rule or a stale check being rejected, and no example covers it.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md: a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away, an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name, approve-only-after-a-passing-check", "kind": "reference"}
A.4.3. {"problem": "Commits that already have checks_passed true but no checked_at (recorded before this change) can no longer be approved. The precondition's result is undefined or false for them, and there is no migration or fallback.", "where": "SKILL.md, Commit.checked_at (optional) and the checks-pass precondition", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.4.4. {"problem": "The glossary entry for 'change check' puts the freshness rule in a parenthetical at the start of the definition. This is awkward, and it is vague about when the one-day age is measured (at approval, or at record_approval). The 'checked_at' type is a string, yet the rule does arithmetic with it (now - checked_at < 1 day), with no stated time format.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring glossary: change check; Commit.checked_at", "kind": "other"}
A.4.5. {"problem": "The check_commit not-approved reason and the record_check settled precondition are unchanged, and nothing says that re-running the check on a stale commit refreshes checked_at. The reason for checks-pass says to 'run it again', but a rerun is allowed only while the commit is not approved, which is consistent. However, record_check overwrites the checked_at of an earlier passing result with a later failing one, which is not described.", "where": "SKILL.md, record_check and check_commit", "kind": "other"}
A.5.0. {"problem": "The invariant 'approved-commits-passed-their-checks' still only requires checks_passed == true (or wording_only). It does not include the one-day freshness rule that approve_commit and record_approval now enforce, so the invariant and the preconditions disagree about what counts as checked. Also, an approved commit's check ages past a day and the invariant would still hold, while the rule says old checks do not count.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring invariants: approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}
A.5.1. {"problem": "Existing examples set up commits with checks_passed true but no checked_at. Under the new checks-pass precondition (now - checked_at < 1 day), an unset checked_at makes the check fail or be undefined, so these examples would now be denied: 'a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away' (C2 checks_passed true, expects approve allowed) and 'an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name' (starts from checks_passed true). No example covers a stale check (a day or older) being refused and a re-run check being accepted.", "where": "EXAMPLES.md: a-deputy-approves-while-the-owner-is-away, an-approved-commit-gets-a-release-name, approve-only-after-a-passing-check", "kind": "reference"}
A.5.2. {"problem": "The checked_at semantics are underspecified. It is a string, yet it is compared by subtraction with 'now' and '< 1 day'. The record_check effect sets checked_at to 'the time of recording', with no format or clock source. The comparison against an unset checked_at (checks_passed true from before this change) is undefined, so previously checked commits may be silently rejected or error.", "where": "SKILL.md, Commit.checked_at; checks-pass and approved-only-if-checked preconditions", "kind": "other"}
A.5.3. {"problem": "The staleness is applied twice, at approve_commit and again at record_approval, with 'now' evaluated separately. A check that is just under a day old at approval can be a day old by the time the kernel reply runs record_approval, and the approval is then refused after the kernel has already merged into main and tagged. The kernel merges on approve_commit, so a late rejection of the recording leaves main and the record out of sync.", "where": "SKILL.md, record_approval precondition approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
A.5.4. {"problem": "The glossary entry for 'change check' puts the freshness rule in a parenthetical before the definition, and the entry's wording-only sentence is unchanged. The freshness rule is not reflected in the check_commit precondition or in the 'approval' wording elsewhere, and the 'checks-pass' reason text is long and mixes in the wording-only rule. Cosmetic, but the glossary now reads oddly.", "where": "SKILL.md, Authoring glossary: change check", "kind": "other"}

