C.1.0. {"problem": "Commits that were checked before this change have checks_passed true but no checked_at. The new rule treats them as unchecked, so they cannot be approved until someone re-runs the check. The world fixture C3 (checks_passed: true, no checked_at) is now unapprovable too. No scenario covers the new freshness rule (stale or missing checked_at denied, fresh check approved), so the change is untested.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: approve_commit checks-pass; world fixture Commit C3; scenarios", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.1.1. {"problem": "record_approval is the kernel's reply to approve_commit and repeats the freshness test. If the check turns 24 hours old between the owner's approval and the kernel recording it, the record is refused. The merge and tag have already been requested, so the commit could be merged to main but never recorded as approved, and then cannot be delivered.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: record_approval approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.1.2. {"problem": "The invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks still requires only checks_passed == true, without checked_at. Its reason says only 'passed its change check'. It no longer matches the tightened approval rules, so the declared guarantee is weaker than what is enforced.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "contradiction"}
C.1.3. {"problem": "The rules assume now() returns seconds, since they compare against 86400, and checked_at is stored as an integer. If now() returns milliseconds or another unit, the window would be about 86 seconds instead of a day. The unit is not stated anywhere in the declaration.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: checks-pass and approved-only-if-checked (now() - target.checked_at < 86400)", "kind": "other"}
C.1.4. {"problem": "The rewritten rules keep the old provenance (interview L6 and L11, 'agree to all recommendations') and status: confirmed. Those quotes predate the one-day requirement and do not support it, so a new rule is marked confirmed without its own confirmation. The glossary entry 'change check' and the version number (16) were also left unchanged.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml: approve_commit checks-pass source, record_approval source, glossary 'change check', version", "kind": "other"}
C.2.0. {"problem": "record_approval is the kernel's reply to approve_commit, and it re-evaluates the freshness check with now() when it runs. approve_commit has already merged the commit into main and tagged it by then. A commit approved shortly before its check turns 24 hours old can have its approval record refused, leaving main merged and tagged while the commit never shows approved. Freshness should be enforced only at the human approval step, or the record step should not re-check the age.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action record_approval, precondition approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.2.1. {"problem": "Commits whose check passed before this change have no checked_at, so exists(target.checked_at) is false. They can no longer be approved until someone re-runs check_commit. The fixture C3 (checks_passed: true, no checked_at) is one such commit. No scenario covers a stale or missing checked_at, and the existing approve scenario never exercises the new age limit.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, world fixtures C1/C3 and scenario approve-only-after-a-passing-check", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.2.2. {"problem": "The invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks and its reason still state the old rule (checks_passed == true, with no age limit). It is now weaker than the approval preconditions, so the declared rule is no longer the whole rule. The glossary entry 'change check' and 'approval' also do not mention that a check expires after a day.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks; glossary change check/approval", "kind": "reference"}
C.2.3. {"problem": "The rewritten preconditions checks-pass and approved-only-if-checked keep status: confirmed and their old sources (the interview quotes 'agree to all recommendations' and 'A for 25 and 26'). Those quotes predate the one-day freshness rule and do not support it, so a rule that nobody confirmed is marked as confirmed. The change should be marked proposed, or cite new evidence.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, approve_commit.checks-pass and record_approval.approved-only-if-checked (source/status)", "kind": "other"}
C.2.4. {"problem": "The rule hard-codes 86400 and assumes now() returns integer seconds, but checked_at is typed integer and nothing declares now()'s unit. If now() returns milliseconds, the window shrinks to about 86 seconds. The same magic number is duplicated in two places.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, checked_at property and the 86400 comparisons", "kind": "other"}
C.2.5. {"problem": "The commit interface gains a new property and a stricter approval rule, but the domain version stays at 16 and nothing in the diff bumps it. Fixture commits carry interface versions such as 1.2.0, and the composition pins authoring interface 1.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml version; toprope-self.composition.yaml members.authoring", "kind": "other"}
C.3.0. {"problem": "record_approval is the kernel's record of an approval that has already happened (merge into main and tag, which the glossary says cannot be undone). The change makes it re-evaluate the 24-hour window with a fresh now(). If the check was, say, 23h59m old when the owner approved and the kernel replies a little later, or the reply is retried, the record is refused. main then holds the commit while the Commit never gets approved=true, so deliver stays blocked and the state is inconsistent. Freshness should be enforced only at approve_commit, not when recording the outcome.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, action record_approval, precondition approved-only-if-checked", "kind": "behaviour"}
C.3.1. {"problem": "The invariant still says an approved commit just 'passed its change check' (checks_passed == true or wording_only). It does not mention freshness. Its reason text no longer matches the two rules that were changed, so the declaration now states the approval rule in two different ways.", "where": "authoring.domain.yaml, invariant approved-commits-passed-their-checks", "kind": "reference"}
